it seems like Nintendo actually listened  to our prayers because the SNES classic  edition is finally coming and it's  packed with so many incredible games  from Super Mario World 2 Link to the  Past to earthbound it's really hard to  complain about the lineup with that  being said here are six games a few of  the GameSpot editors wish the NES  classic had if your favorite game is on  the list put it in the comments below  and consider subscribing for more videos  like this one  although the IP is all but dead  actraiser is a piece of gaming history  that truly represents the experimental  innovation that occurred and the SNES is  hey day while there were plenty of  action platformers and a surprising  number of top-down strategy games  actraiser was the only one that  definitely combined the two genres and  it featured a framed story that  positioned you as the creator and  protector of the world who needed to  appease his followers in order to gain  more power actraiser did get a sequel  three years later though it is skewed  the strategy element entirely and  outside of releases on the Virtual  Console and mobile the entire franchise  has lain dormant but this quirky  mishmash deserved a spot on the SNES  classic pilotwings isn't a Nintendo  property that gets a lot of love these  days but when the SNES debuted the  original pilotwings was an impressive  game that offered a lot of variety in a  single package under the guise of  earning various pilots licenses you were  thrust into the cockpits of planes into  the harness of a skydiving parachute and  even buckled into a jetpack each  scenario presents of unique controls and  objectives but it was the intuitive and  thoughtful controls that made the  experience a pleasure even when you fail  repeatedly while perfecting your  piloting techniques a game about  learning how to pilot various  contraptions doesn't sound exciting but  the use of the NES is mode 7 feature  gave pilot wings environments a sense of  depth that was unparalleled and console  gaming at the time it was easy to  respect the work pile of wings did to  differentiate the Super Nintendo from  the competition and it's disappointing  to hear that it won't be a part of the  SNES classic  Earthworm Jim may not be as iconic as  Mario and Sonic but the game he hails  from combined platforming and 2d gunplay  in a fun and uniquely bizarre package  the premise is that a spacesuit crash  lands onto earth and gives a normal  earthworm superhuman strength earthworm  jim used a cool art style coupled with  excellent hand-drawn animations visually  the game has aged relatively well it  also featured some crazy batch levels  that brought you to a planet made of  mucus and a stage where you're  navigating your way through intestines  the gunplay allows you to shoot in eight  different directions and the platforming  allows you to jump and swing from ledge  to ledge using your earthworm head as  the lasso the game spawned three sequels  and a children's cartoon show and  deserves to be played if you're a fan of  90s style platformers while the NES  classic edition included Tecmo Bowl the  SNES classic leaves out its platform's  most iconic sports game NBA Jam it's a  game that was just plain old-fashioned  fun two-on-two basketball where players  catch on fire when they're doing well  knock each other down to steal the ball  and generally ignore NBA rules making  for an incredibly entertaining  experience the basketball games appeal  extended even to non basketball fans its  arcade style gameplay is approachable  and doesn't require in-depth knowledge  of the real world sport add in the  over-the-top commentary and multiplayer  friendly nature of the game an NBA Jam  was seemingly tailor-made for inclusion  as an SNES classic release  the SNES was one of many consoles that  brought an arcade experience to the  living room but SNES games like super  r-type had selling points beyond its  similarities to their arcade  counterparts part port part remix super  r-type took four stages from r-type to  and introduced three new areas much of  super are types appeal is how well it  retains the original games engaging  weapon system some weapon pickups are  more useful than others in specific  situations and environments and one way  of mastering an R type game is knowing  when to use a specific armament and  where to obtain it this also speaks to  super are types high difficulty which  would have complemented the equally  challenging super ghouls and ghosts a  game that has been confirmed for the  SNES classic the Super Nintendo is home  to many beloved RPGs including the  charmingly odd earthbound and of course  Final Fantasy 6 both of which enjoy a  spot on the list of games bundled in the  SNES classic while I can't really point  a finger at Nintendo for ignoring RPGs I  can at least begrudge that Chrono  Trigger won't be coming along for the  ride Chrono Trigger is regarded by many  to be one of the best console RPGs ever  made it comprised an unusual time-travel  mechanic had a complex combat system  that allowed you to strategically  combine character abilities and had a  really memorable soundtrack  [Music]  and all of these are valid reasons to  continue playing the game today 22 years  after its release Chrono Trigger also  represents an interesting crossroads for  square in Enix years before the two  companies merged Chrono Trigger was  devised by the leaders of two competing  RPG behemoths Yuji Horii of Dragon Quest  and Hironobu Sakaguchi of Final Fantasy  and had additional input from the  creator of Dragon Ball Z Akira Toriyama  an interesting touchstone in an  all-around lovely game Chrono Trigger  absolutely deserves a spot in the SNES  classic editions library  [Music]  you
 

